// Lesson 4.3 — Chase: The Aggro Radius
// SOLO BUILD (scaffold level 4) — the second rung-4 lesson in the course, and
// like 3.2 it hands over no code and no API table. Every part of this feature
// has been built once already, somewhere else: the radius is 3.1's distance
// comparison, closing on the player is 4.1's movement-toward-a-target, and
// facing the way it walks is 4.2's Direction value. The one genuinely new
// part — and the part the lesson tells the student to slow down on — is
// deriving a direction from two positions that are BOTH moving, rather than
// from input or from a target the student placed.
// The design problem on top of the maths is still the PATROL -> CHASE
// TRANSITION, and the bug that comes from letting both behaviours write the
// same transform. The three coexistence designs stay in the body because the
// bug hunt, the socratic and the specification all ask the student to choose
// between them rather than to invent them from nothing.
//
// GROUND TRUTH: EnemyController.cs ships as an empty stub — by now it holds
// the patrol the student wrote in 4.1, and everything in this lesson is an
// addition to that file. The MobileEnemy prefab exists FOUR times in the
// Overworld, and carries no Collider2D and no Rigidbody2D unless 4.1 added them.
//
// The parts below are NOT rendered at level 4 (see src/lib/decompose.ts) —
// they are written out because they are the record of the intended split, and
// because they are what the lesson hands over verbatim if it is ever taught at
// a lower rung. Do not delete them as "unused".
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'An enemy that comes for me when I get close. That is the whole feature, and like every ' +
    'feature in this course it is not one problem — it is several standing in a line, one of ' +
    'which you have never solved before.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Find the player once, and hold on to them',
      does:
        'Get a reference to the player at startup and keep it, with a guard for the case where there is no player in the scene at all.',
      doc:
        'Your targeting choice from 3.1 — FindWithTag or a serialized reference — and the null guard that has to follow either one.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Measure how far away they are',
      does: 'Ask for the straight-line distance between the enemy and the player, every frame.',
      doc:
        'Vector3.Distance against sqrMagnitude — the same comparison you chose between for the turret in 3.1.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide that close enough means chase',
      does:
        'Compare that distance against a designer-tunable aggro radius and set the enemy\'s mode from the answer.',
      doc:
        'The range condition, and the boundary you argued about at the exact radius, in 3.1. The tuning knob itself is the serialized field you have used since 1.4.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Derive a direction from the two positions',
      does:
        'Subtract the enemy\'s position from the player\'s, in that order, and give the result length one — every frame, because both ends are moving.',
      doc:
        'The genuinely new part. The subtraction and the normalizing are the pair you aimed a projectile with in 3.2; what is new is doing it continuously, at a target you do not control.',
      href: '3.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Move the enemy along that direction',
      does: 'Multiply the direction by a speed and move the transform, frame-rate independently.',
      doc:
        'The movement method you chose for the patrol in 4.1 — the same call, pointed at a target that moves.',
      href: '4.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Face the way it is chasing',
      does:
        'Set the animator\'s Direction value from the direction of travel, so the sprite reads as a hunter instead of a guard.',
      doc:
        'The enemy animator you drove in 4.2 — the four Direction values, and the controller that has no idle state to fall back on.',
      href: '4.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Stop walking the beat while the chase runs',
      does:
        'Guarantee that exactly one behaviour writes the enemy\'s position in any given frame, and decide what the beat remembers when the chase ends.',
      doc:
        'The patrol you wrote in 4.1 — its direction field and the anchor it turns around.',
      href: '4.1.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Most of the parts inside this feature are things you have already built somewhere else: a ' +
    'distance check written for a turret, a movement toward a target written for a guard, an ' +
    'animator value driven from a direction, a tuning knob, a null guard. Only one part has no ' +
    'earlier version, and it is the one to slow down on — a direction worked out from two ' +
    'positions, one of which you do not control, recomputed every frame. That is what a ' +
    'well-sequenced course feels like from the inside: the same parts, standing in a new order, ' +
    'with one genuinely new one among them.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the enemy chase me" cannot be looked up. "Subtract two positions to get a ' +
    'direction" can. If a part is still too big to search for, it is two parts.',
  connect: [
    'The aggro radius is a lesson you have already finished, wearing a new name. Which lesson did you compare a distance against a tuned number in — and what object was on the other end of that comparison?',
    'Your enemy already knows how to move toward a target, and it learned that a lesson ago. Where did that target come from — and what is different about this one?',
    'Exactly one part of this problem has no earlier version anywhere in the course. Which part is it, and what do you not yet know about it?',
    'One lesson ago you made the enemy sprite face the way it walks. When the enemy is chasing you, whose position is that value derived from — and why does a direction that changes every frame not surprise that animator?',
  ],
  turns: [
    'Write the rule down before you code it: when the enemy is inside its patrol beat and inside the aggro radius at the same time, which behaviour wins? Say what the player sees if you pick the other one.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>4.3 — Chase: The Aggro Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Patrol is calm. One distance check turns a guard into a hunter — and you have written that
    distance check before, for the turret. What makes today different is not the maths. It is that
    the thing on the other end of the measurement is <em>moving</em>, and that the enemy has to
    stop doing what it was doing in order to come after you.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make an enemy chase me?', 4)}

${renderDecompose('4.3', DECOMPOSE, 4)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: Noticing You</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Your goal: the enemy should come for you when you get close, and go back to its
        beat when you leave.</strong> That is the whole feature — and this is the test for it.
        Every line below is something you can watch happen in the editor with the Console open and
        the Scene view in front of you. You are done when all of them are true at once.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>You stand well outside the enemy's radius and it <strong>ignores you completely</strong>
            — it walks its beat exactly as it did before this lesson.</li>
        <li>You cross the line and <strong>it comes for you</strong>: it drops the beat and starts
            closing, smoothly, without waiting for a turn.</li>
        <li>You step sideways while it is closing and <strong>it steers</strong>. Its path curves
            toward you instead of committing to the spot where it first noticed you.</li>
        <li>You walk back out past the line and <strong>it gives up</strong> and walks a beat
            again. You can point at which way it heads first, and say why it heads there.</li>
        <li>It <strong>never stutters and never crabs sideways</strong> while it is chasing you.</li>
        <li>With <strong>no Player in the scene at all</strong>, nothing errors and the enemy keeps
            walking its beat.</li>
        <li>The Console prints <strong>which mode the enemy is in</strong>, and that word flips the
            instant you cross the line — in both directions.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        <strong>Now the parts.</strong> Seven of them, and six are lessons you have already
        finished. The radius is <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>'s distance comparison, pointed at the
        same player the turret was watching. Closing on you is <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>'s
        movement toward a target, pointed at something you did not place. Facing the way it walks
        is <a href="4.2.html">4.2</a>'s animator value, set by code you have already written. There
        is no new API in this lesson at all — it is three old lessons standing in a new order, with
        one part inside it that has never existed before.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Find the player, once, and keep them.</strong> A reference held from startup,
            plus the guard for a scene with no player in it. (<a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Measure how far away they are.</strong> The distance between two positions,
            re-asked every frame. (<a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Decide that close enough means chase.</strong> A comparison against a number a
            designer can tune from the Inspector. (<a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Work out which way to go.</strong> A direction derived from two positions, both
            of which are moving, recomputed every frame. <strong>This part is new.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Move along it.</strong> Whichever movement call you chose for the patrol.
            (<a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Face the way it goes.</strong> The animator value, driven this time by a chase
            instead of a beat. (<a href="4.2.html">4.2</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Stop the beat while the chase runs.</strong> One writer to the enemy's position,
            and a decision about what the beat remembers when the chase ends.
            (<a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>)</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Slow down on one part: working out the direction.</strong> Every direction you have
        written so far came from somewhere that was handed to you. In <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a> it
        came from the keys you were pressing. In <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a> it was a sign flip — one
        fixed axis, chosen once and never reconsidered. In <a href="3.2.html">3.2</a> you subtracted
        two positions, but only at the instant of firing, from a muzzle you had placed yourself. This
        time both ends move: the enemy is walking, you are running, and the direction has to be
        worked out again from nothing but the two positions, on every frame. That is the genuinely
        new part of this feature, and it is where the bugs will be.

        <p>
            The one fact I will hand you, so you can spend your attention on the rest: the vector
            from the enemy to the player is the player's position minus the enemy's, <em>in that
            order</em>. Swap the operands and you have built something that runs away instead. And
            that vector has to be given length one before you multiply it by a speed — otherwise the
            enemy's speed depends on how far away you are, and it crawls the moment it reaches you.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p>
        The second decision is not about maths at all. Your <code>Update()</code> method currently
        patrols unconditionally, and now it has to be able to chase as well — which means two
        behaviours want the same object. There is no earlier lesson to copy an answer from here, so
        read the three options and pick one on purpose:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>How the two behaviours coexist</th><th>What the code does each frame</th><th>What the player sees</th><th>The risk</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; patrol is the <code>else</code></strong></td>
            <td>Exactly one of the two runs.</td>
            <td>A clean switch: the moment you cross the line, the enemy turns and comes for you.</td>
            <td>Nothing holds the answer to "am I chasing?" — so no other part of your code can ask. It also forces you to decide which wins when you are inside the patrol beat <em>and</em> the radius at the same time.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; the patrol keeps running anyway</strong></td>
            <td>Both write to the enemy's position in the same frame.</td>
            <td>A stutter, or an enemy that drifts toward you while still dutifully flipping at the end of its beat.</td>
            <td>Two systems moving one object. Their sum is a third movement that <em>nobody designed</em> — and it is the single most common bug in this lesson.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; the patrol is switched off by a flag</strong></td>
            <td>The flag records the mode, and the patrol checks it.</td>
            <td>Same as the first row, at first.</td>
            <td>One extra field, and one extra thing to keep true. Harmless for two behaviours — and the seed of a real problem once you have four. Keep this thought for <a href="4.5.html">4.5</a>.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The bug you are most likely to write today.</strong> If your patrol code still
        runs while the chase runs, the enemy receives two movement instructions per frame. With
        the <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a> leash approach this is especially easy to miss, because
        the patrol's flip check is still measuring distance from the spawn anchor and happily
        reversing your chase mid-pursuit. Watch the enemy's motion: a hunter that stutters or
        crabs sideways is not chasing badly, it is patroling and chasing at once.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: States, Transitions and Two Writers</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You gave the turret a distance check in 3.1 and it opened fire. What does the enemy do with that same answer that the turret did not do — and why does that make the enemy harder to build?</li>
            <li>Your enemy is inside its patrol beat and inside the aggro radius at the same time. Which behaviour must win? Describe exactly what the player sees if you pick the other one.</li>
            <li>If both behaviours move the transform in one frame, what is the sum of "walk right at 2 units per second" and "walk toward the player at 2 units per second"? Why is that sum not a chase — and which direction does the enemy actually travel?</li>
            <li><code>Vector3.Distance</code> and <code>sqrMagnitude</code> answer the same question. Which would you use for a number you intend to print to the Console for a designer to tune, and which would you use if this enemy were copied two hundred times across a level?</li>
            <li>You chose a targeting approach for the turret in 3.1. The enemy prefab exists as <strong>four</strong> instances in the Overworld. Does that change your answer? What does a serialized reference cost you per instance?</li>
            <li>Where does the target lookup go so it runs exactly once? And what happens to a cached reference if your death rule from <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> reloads the scene — is the enemy's cached player still the player?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Turn the Guard Into a Hunter</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Open <code>Assets/Scripts/EnemyController.cs</code> — the patrol you wrote in
        <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>. Everything today is an addition to that file. Save the scene
        first: you are about to give four enemies an opinion about you.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for the chase:</strong>

        <p><strong>Acquiring the target:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which of the two targeting options from <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a> did you choose, and what does that choice cost across four enemy instances?</li>
            <li>Which lifecycle method holds the lookup so it runs once rather than every frame? What did you do in 3.1, and is the reason the same?</li>
            <li>What does <code>FindWithTag</code> return when nothing matches, and what guard clause must follow it?</li>
            <li>Should the guard clause stop the whole update, or only the chase half? What does the enemy need to keep doing when there is no player in the scene?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The aggro knob:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What serialized float lets a designer set how close you must get before the enemy turns? What do you name it so a stranger reading the Inspector understands the relationship to the patrol knob beside it?</li>
            <li>Choose a default and justify it against the size of the Overworld, your patrol beat, and the turret's detection range from <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>. Which of those two radii should be larger, and what does the player feel if it is the other way round?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The pursuit:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which subtraction order gives you a vector pointing <em>from</em> the enemy <em>to</em> the player? State it, then say what the other order produces.</li>
            <li>Why must that vector be normalized before you multiply it by a speed? Describe the enemy's approach curve without it — an enemy that slows down as it closes is not a hunter.</li>
            <li>Which movement call moves the enemy, and is it the same one your patrol uses? If the patrol and the chase use different movement methods, what does the Rigidbody2D have to satisfy for both?</li>
            <li>Both positions are moving now. Where in the frame does the direction have to be worked out so that the enemy is steering at where you are, rather than at where you were?</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The transition — the problem with no earlier lesson to copy:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Which of the three coexistence designs did you choose? Write it down before you code it, including what happens at exactly the aggro boundary.</li>
            <li>What guarantees that only one of the two behaviours moves the enemy in any given frame? Point at the exact construct in your own file.</li>
            <li>Should the patrol's own memory — its direction and its anchor — be reset or preserved when the chase ends? What does the player see if you preserve it, and what do they see if you reset it?</li>
            <li>Which animator <code>Direction</code> value should be set while chasing, and how does the enemy's animator cope with a direction that changes every frame? (Re-read your <a href="4.2.html">4.2</a> notes rather than guessing.)</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Proving it:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>How do you print the current mode to the Console so you can watch the transition happen? What word would you print for each mode?</li>
            <li>Which of the test lines at the top of this lesson can you tick off with the Console alone, and which ones need the Scene view as well?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p><strong>When you are stuck, this is the order to reread:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><a href="3.1.html">3.1</a> — the distance check, the radius, and the comparison at the boundary</li>
        <li><a href="4.1.html">4.1</a> — your patrol: the beat, the anchor and the direction field it already keeps</li>
        <li><a href="4.2.html">4.2</a> — the enemy animator, its four <code>Direction</code> values, and why a wrong value is silent</li>
        <li><a href="3.2.html">3.2</a> — the last time you subtracted two positions to get a direction</li>
        <li><a href="1.5.html">1.5</a> — vectors and normalizing, where the diagonal bug was fixed</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — when you know the name and need the exact spelling</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        That list is the point of the lesson. When you have forgotten how to normalize a vector, you
        do not ask a person and you do not guess — you go and read the lesson where you already did
        it. A working programmer spends most of the day reading, and this list is what "reading the
        documentation for each part" looks like when the documentation is your own course.
    </p>

    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>Test the mode before you test the movement.</strong> Print the mode, walk across
        the boundary, and watch the word change before you touch anything else. A hunter that never
        gives up and a guard that never started look identical when you are standing in the wrong
        place — the Console is what tells the two apart.
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and play. Walk toward an enemy from a distance and watch the Console. You should see
        the mode change the instant you cross the boundary, and change back when you walk away —
        and while chasing, the enemy's movement should be smooth and single-minded.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The Broken Hunter</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Reverse the subtraction order in the pursuit vector. What does the enemy do, and what does that prove about which operand must come first?</li>
            <li>Delete the null guard and remove the Player from the scene. Which method errors, and which line does Unity point at?</li>
            <li>Leave the patrol code running while the chase runs. What does the enemy's motion look like — and how would you name that bug in terms of two writers to one transform?</li>
            <li>Set the aggro radius larger than the whole map. What has "chasing" become — and why is a radius that big exactly the same as deleting the patrol?</li>
            <li>Set the enemy's speed higher than your player's. Can the player escape at all? What does that do to the fairness contract you designed in 2.2?</li>
            <li>Walk slowly back and forth across the aggro boundary and read the Console. What is it printing sixty times a second, and which single number would you change to calm it down?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Aggro Design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Give the chase a memory: once the enemy has seen you, it keeps coming even outside the radius, until you get <code>12</code> units away. What new field does that need, and does the AP exam's word "state" apply to it?</li>
            <li>With two different distances — one to start chasing, one to give up — walk across the boundary again. How has the Console flicker changed, and why does a gap between two thresholds remove it?</li>
            <li>Should walls stop the chase? Name the Unity feature you would need (look up <code>Physics2D.Raycast</code>), and explain why running it every frame for every enemy is expensive. What could you do instead to run it less often?</li>
            <li>Set the aggro radius slightly larger than the turret's detection range and place an enemy and a turret near each other. What combined kill zone did you design, and where is the one safe line through it?</li>
            <li>Commit and push: <code>"4.3: aggro radius + chase"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Break the chase into parts, and say in one line what each part does</li>
            <li>Name, for each part, the earlier lesson you already built it in</li>
            <li>Compare <code>Vector3.Distance</code> against <code>sqrMagnitude</code>, and say which you chose for the enemy and why</li>
            <li>Compare <code>FindWithTag</code> against a serialized reference, and account for the four enemy instances</li>
            <li>State which subtraction order produces a pursuit vector, and why normalizing matters</li>
            <li>Explain your patrol-to-chase transition, and point at the construct that stops both behaviours running at once</li>
            <li>Explain why two writers on one transform produce motion nobody designed</li>
            <li>Design an aggro radius that reads as fair next to the turret's range</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I…? index</a> — every question this course answers, in the words you would search with</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — chase and pursuit in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — patrol and chase as two states of one machine</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — <code>normalized</code>, <code>FindWithTag</code> and vector maths</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.4.html">4.4 — Attack: Close-Range Contact Damage</a>, where catching you finally has a consequence.</p>
</div>`;
